  Hand-derived (not run through lirc2xml/DecodeIR) from a real NEC1 decode
  of the Topping RC-15A remote (bundled with several Topping DAC/amp
  models) reported by forum user halfSpinDoctor on Audio Science Review:
  https://www.audiosciencereview.com/forum/index.php?threads/remote-codes-for-topping.10708/

  Every one of these 8 captured 32-bit words passes the standard NEC
  address/command complement check (byte1==~byte0, byte3==~byte2), which
  is strong evidence they are genuine captures rather than typos. NEC1
  timing is a fixed public standard (unlike Sony SIRC's per-device
  addressing), so this carries far less uncertainty than the Sony BD work.
  decoding device/subdevice/obc/hex0 below are the raw captured address/
  command bytes (not bit-reversed to match DecodeIR's internal, undocumented
  convention - see the Sony files in this repo for why that isn't feasible
  without running DecodeIR itself).
-->
